  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nMidday radar and satellite imagery depicted the remnant mid\nlevel vorticity max from Pacific Hurricane Polo, moving\nnortheast across SW KS. Widespread moderate to heavy rain was\nassociated with this feature. Primary closed, extratropical\ncyclone was still back over New Mexico. Surface analysis\ndepicted the primary surface cyclone over the NW Panhandles,\nexpected to translate into the southeast zones this afternoon.\nMesoanalysis already depicts enough bulk shear and storm\nrelative helicity to support rotating updrafts, favoring the\nsoutheast counties, with meaningful instability poised south of\nthe Oklahoma border (CAPE > 1000 J/kg). Through the afternoon,\na midlevel dryslot will work its way north into SW KS, pushing\nstratiform rain north, allowing instability and clearing to\nspread north, and encourage rapidly redeveloping scattered\nthunderstorms to develop after 2 pm. With extremely low LCLs,\nand a saturated tropical environment and strong low level shear,\nweak tropical tornadoes are expected. These are not the same\nanimals as supercell tornadoes of our spring seasons, but are \nstill capable of causing minor damage, and any tornado watches \nand warnings still need to be respected through this evening. \nSpecial weather statements highlighting weak tornadoes may also \nbe issued from DDC for some of these storms. \n\nTonight, as the hurricane remnants eject north into Nebraska and\nIowa, the extratropical cutoff midlevel cyclone will be ejecting\nnortheastward into the western zones through sunrise Thursday.\nAlthough chance category pops remain tonight, to catch any\nscattered activity, a significant lull in activity is expected\ntonight as the midlevel dry slot prevails. Widespread\ndeformation rainfall is expected to be confined to southeast\nColorado and adjacent far western zones through 7 am Thursday. \n\nModels forecast the closed midlevel cyclone to weaken into an\nopen shortwave trough, and eject eastward directly over Kansas\ndaylight Thursday. Guidance continues to trend slightly\nsouthward with the primary deformation lift Thursday, and pop\ngrids remain in the likely/definite categories much of Thursday\nfor many zones as a result. Another 1/2 to 1 inch of rain is\nprobable Thursday, especially where the preferred deformation\naxis sets up. Associated cold front arrives Thursday afternoon\nwith increasing/elevated northeast winds, and temperatures\nstruggling to get out of the 60s. \n\nSubsidence takes over rapidly Thursday night, with a clear sky\nand strong surface ridging building south into Kansas through\nsunrise Friday. Friday promises to deliver a beautiful fall\nafternoon, with sunshine, temperatures in the 60s, and light\nwinds. A clear sky, light winds, and 1025 mb surface ridging\nwill provide strong radiational cooling Friday night and\nSaturday morning, providing the coolest temperatures so far \nthis young fall season. NBM temperatures in the 40s Saturday\nmorning were accepted. A long stretch of dry weather is expected\nthis weekend through next week, with a benign synoptic pattern\nand ridging prevailing over the Rockies.",
